"""The stages of a run, in the order a run reaches them.

A manifest key becomes required at the stage whose key struct declares it,
and the value here names that stage. The correspondence is structural:
the struct declaring a key IS the statement of its stage, so a key cannot
be given a type in one place and a stage in another."""
enum RunStage {
  PRE_FLIGHT: "pre-flight"
  SCOPE: "scope"
  PLAN: "plan"
  DISPATCH: "dispatch"
  RETRO: "retro"
}

"""How much reasoning effort the harness was set to for this run.

A harness that exposes no such setting records it as unrecorded rather
than leaving the key absent, so a reader can tell "not offered" from
"not yet written"."""
enum RunEffort {
  LOW: "low"
  MEDIUM: "medium"
  HIGH: "high"
  XHIGH: "xhigh"
  UNRECORDED: "unrecorded"
}

"""Where an authorization row's value came from.

A row resolved from the project's own configuration and a row that took
the skill's default are different facts, and the record exists to stop a
decision being re-litigated mid-run."""
enum AuthSource {
  PROJECT: "sf.toml"
  DEFAULT: "default"
}

"""An authorization row whose resolved value is a word."""
struct TextAuthRow {
  value: String
  source: AuthSource
}

"""An authorization row whose resolved value is yes or no."""
struct FlagAuthRow {
  value: Bool
  source: AuthSource
}

"""An authorization row whose resolved value is a count."""
struct CountAuthRow {
  value: Int
  source: AuthSource
}

"""The authorization gate's rows, each with its resolved value and where
that value came from.

Named fields rather than a map, because the rows are a fixed set and each
one's value has a fixed type: a map would have to carry a value of mixed
type, and there is no such thing in a closed schema. This is also what
lets the wire form stay exactly what the skills write today."""
struct StandingAuth {
  """draft, ready, or none — what the run opens at the end."""
  pull_request: TextAuthRow
  """Whether a bead belonging to another epic is moved into this run."""
  move_foreign_beads: FlagAuthRow
  """Whether review findings are worked in this run or only filed."""
  admit_review_findings: FlagAuthRow
  """once or never — whether a repeated failure escalates a tier."""
  tier_escalation: TextAuthRow
  """How many review loops the run may spend."""
  review_budget: CountAuthRow
  """Whether the run may merge to the default branch."""
  merge_to_default: FlagAuthRow
}

"""The model behind each capability tier, least to most capable."""
struct TierModels {
  light: String
  standard: String
  advanced: String
  frontier: String
}

"""A bead the run considered and did not admit."""
struct Exclusion {
  id: String
  reason: String
}

"""A bead the run moved into its own epic, and where it came from."""
struct Move {
  id: String
  from: String
}

"""Keys the run's pre-flight establishes.

Every key here is optional on the wire: the manifest is a partial record
that grows as a run proceeds, and a key's absence before its stage is a
legitimate state rather than a defect. What this struct asserts is the
stage at which each key becomes required, which is the fact validation
reads."""
struct PreFlightKeys {
  """The ids of the checks the project declares, in declaration order."""
  checks_ids: [String]?
  """What the declared checks printed on the untouched tree, verbatim.

  Recorded whole rather than summarized: the bar for every bead in the
  run is "no findings beyond this", which a summary cannot express."""
  checks_baseline: String?
  """The status the baseline run exited with. Zero means every check is
  expected to pass, so any failure a bead sees is its own."""
  checks_baseline_status: Int?
  """The most dispatches the run may have in flight at once."""
  concurrency_cap: Int?
  """The model behind each capability tier."""
  models: TierModels?
  """The reasoning effort the harness was set to."""
  effort: RunEffort?
  """Facts established this run that no dispatch should re-diagnose,
  each naming the command that proved it and the result observed.

  Append-only: dispatch prompts cite entries by position, so an entry
  removed or reordered retargets every citation that addressed it."""
  ledger: [String]?
}

"""Keys the run's scope and admission establish."""
struct ScopeKeys: PreFlightKeys {
  """The flat target epic whose task children are the run's work."""
  target: String?
  """Every bead admitted to the run."""
  admitted: [String]?
  """Every bead considered and not admitted, with why."""
  excluded: [Exclusion]?
  """Every bead moved into the run's epic, and where it came from."""
  moved: [Move]?
  """The authorization gate's resolved rows."""
  standing_auth: StandingAuth?
  """How many review loops the run may spend."""
  review_budget: Int?
  """The commit trailers the run resolved.

  Optional at every stage, not merely until its own: a run that resolved
  no trailer convention legitimately carries none, and an absent value
  here means that rather than "not yet written"."""
  trailers: String?
}

"""Keys the run's planning establishes."""
struct PlanKeys: ScopeKeys {
  """Where the edge document the run was built from was written."""
  collision_edges: String?
  """What became of the partition the planner proposed."""
  partition_preview: String?
}

"""Keys the run establishes when it creates its branch, immediately
before its first dispatch."""
struct DispatchKeys: PlanKeys {
  """The sprint branch. Written once: a run whose manifest already
  carries it reuses the branch and creates nothing, which is what makes
  a resumed run land where the original did."""
  branch: String?
  """The commit the sprint branch was cut from, recorded at creation and
  never derived later — a merge-base taken afterwards assumes the base
  has not moved, and on a long run it will have."""
  base_sha: String?
  """How many review loops the run has spent. Durable because a resumed
  run that restarted the counter would loop forever."""
  review_passes: Int?
}

"""Keys the run's retrospective establishes.

An absent findings_path means the retro did not run, which is a different
result from a retro that found nothing."""
struct RetroKeys: DispatchKeys {
  """Where the run's one human-facing document was written."""
  findings_path: String?
  """How many findings became issues."""
  retro_filed: Int?
  """How many findings a human declined."""
  retro_declined: Int?
  """How many findings were routed elsewhere."""
  retro_routed: Int?
}

"""The run manifest: the JSON object in a run epic's tracker metadata,
and the run's only durable record.

This is the whole declared key set. A key outside it is refused rather
than stored, and a value whose type is not its key's declared type is
refused, which is what the closed shape above expresses.

The stage at which a key becomes required is the first struct in the
chain that declares it — PreFlightKeys, then ScopeKeys, PlanKeys,
DispatchKeys, RetroKeys — so a key's name, its type and its stage all
come from one declaration."""
struct RunManifest: RetroKeys {
}
